{"version":"0.12.13","prev_tag":"v0.12.12","target":"default","tag_prefix":"","area_scope":[],"commits":[{"sha":"6de5fd727816426b5171e7ce4fd86e3abf2eb186","title":"Restore roadmap and status when persisted Task values are unknown — status: preserve released Task PRs without copy (#1219)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/children.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/fixtures/store_0_12_8_task_pr.sql","rust/loopflow/tests/status_tests.rs"]},{"sha":"9151b159623fc98e9f6bc0c21c78944c8e1285aa","title":"Restore Task launch from User control turns — restore runtime context for Work bodies (#1214)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/bin/lf.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/agent.rs","rust/loopflow/src/flowloop/wave.rs","rust/loopflow/src/journal/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/run.rs","rust/loopflow/src/project/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs"]},{"sha":"76717dcc87a50a3a97c5ec71509d00216f26c99f","title":"Preserve human reviewer mode in Task Ask sessions (#1222)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs"]},{"sha":"10f8843eca995f66ee38e4515ea9500e4dfe0ce0","title":"sessions: add repo-scoped ghostty multiplexer (#1221)","files":["swift/Loopflow/Models/MultiplexerLayout.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/MultiplexerStore.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/RegistryQuery.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/LoopflowApp.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PodiumModel.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Ghostty/GhosttyManager.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Ghostty/GhosttyTerminalView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/AskAttentionView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/PodiumView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/SessionsView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkSurfaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/MultiplexerLayoutTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/MultiplexerStoreTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/PodiumModelTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/RegistryQueryTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/SessionsStoreTests.swift","swift/README.md"]},{"sha":"c6c3df92ae712d0a0ee9658e8d064d0c684e2c80","title":"LOO-249: Make Task PR copy explain intent and lifecycle (#1220)","files":["docs/lf.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/ops/skill/loopflow.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/ops/skill/pr-message.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/gate.md","rust/loopflow/src/ops/land.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/pr.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task_pm.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/land_tests.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/pr_tests.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/support/mod.rs"]},{"sha":"93627c753d3145a1d3f8a39f6f504b081ed45fb0","title":"Run the skill as a command in human-gate Ask sessions (not an inlined prompt) — ask: run human flow gates through the skill harness (#1223)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/ops/ask.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs"]},{"sha":"ab39cb83df0e8313e54300fa64fde2b950e14574","title":"Make managed Tasks survive missing lifecycle flows — Park invalid recovery once (#1228)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/ops/task.rs"]},{"sha":"521ae7d3f01f1e46618ab2247ffccdf3f41411d2","title":"Status surfaces must distinguish last recorded event from current truth — status: separate current work truth from history (#1225)","files":["docs/architecture.md","docs/conducting.md","rust/loopflow-test-support/src/lib.rs","rust/loopflow/src/bin/lf.rs","rust/loopflow/src/child.rs","rust/loopflow/src/durable.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/process.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/wave_context.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/context.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/invocation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/runs.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/waves.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/work.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lfd/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/project.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task.rs","rust/loopflow/src/project/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/project/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/children.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/durable.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/migrations.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/migrations/drafts/status_truth__b75f82f77cfb4c8ea2eb4a281c4af03c.sql","rust/loopflow/src/store/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/rows.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/children.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/durable.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/actions.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/relocate.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/types.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave_host.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/dto_fixtures.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/status_tests.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/wave_repository_ownership.rs","swift/Loopflow/Models/ContextLab.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/InvocationSurfaceRecord.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/Wave.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WaveLens.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WaveViewModel.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/WaveWorkMap.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/RegistryQuery.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/MockWaveFixture.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PodiumModel.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/ContextLabView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/PodiumConsole.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/RoadmapView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WaveDetailPane.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkSurfaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/ContextLabTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DTOFixtureTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/MockWaveFixtureTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/PodiumModelTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/ProjectFailureHistoryViewTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/RegistryQueryTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/SessionsStoreTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveLensTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorkAttentionTests.swift","tests/fixtures/dto/context_lab_snapshot.json","tests/fixtures/dto/invocation_surface.json","tests/fixtures/dto/roadmap_snapshot.json","tests/fixtures/dto/task_attention_states.json","tests/fixtures/dto/wave_detail.json"]},{"sha":"ebe2fb596babd13343e38b20089eb98a40cd1985","title":"Keep concurrent trace capture from killing Task Runs — trace: preserve captures under concurrent WAL writes (#1226)","files":["rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/trace.rs"]},{"sha":"347671abf7cc6a311addfa63347b812d239cc6c7","title":"build(deps): bump base64 from 0.22.1 to 0.23.1 (#1218)","files":["Cargo.lock","rust/loopflow/Cargo.toml"]},{"sha":"7efbbf32c0b5ab047b37dc7c753d92a76407f2c2","title":"build(deps): bump fancy-regex from 0.18.0 to 0.19.0 (#1216)","files":["Cargo.lock","rust/loopflow/Cargo.toml"]},{"sha":"1d364f2b5e567d632df4f2a01fa45603035edf85","title":"build(deps-dev): bump ruff from 0.16.0 to 0.16.3 in the python group (#1217)","files":["uv.lock"]},{"sha":"d854d79561e9115458d4a895e4d5c38dd433e1dd","title":"Define Project-owned live metrics for KR-driven Wave health — metrics: add project-owned live evidence (#1224)","files":["docs/agent-api.md","docs/architecture.md","docs/authoring.md","docs/conducting.md","docs/lf.md","docs/waves.md","python/tests/test_lifecycle_scorecard.py","python/tests/test_materialize_rust_tests.py","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/project/skill/project_clarify.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/project/skill/project_mutate.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/project/skill/project_pursue.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/gate.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/kickoff.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/prompt.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/task_clarify.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/task_mutate.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/task/skill/task_pursue.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/scan.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/split-wave.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/update-wave.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/wave_clarify.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/wave_mutate.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/wave_pursue.md","rust/loopflow/src/flowloop/wave.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/waves.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/flow.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/metrics.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/run.rs","rust/loopflow/src/project/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/metrics.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/migrations/drafts/project_metric_observations__8a7dd94340bc4fb9a7836c5dc39e0c21.sql","rust/loopflow/src/store/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/metrics.rs","rust/loopflow/src/task/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/README.md","rust/loopflow/src/wave/metrics.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/dto_fixtures.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/status_tests.rs","scripts/generate_screenshots.py","scripts/lifecycle_scorecard.py","scripts/materialize_rust_tests.py","scripts/screenshots.yaml","swift/Loopflow/Models/MetricPortfolio.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/RegistryQuery.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/WaveOrigin.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/MockWaveFixture.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PodiumModel.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/PortfolioRepoState.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WaveDetailPane.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WavesView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkSurfaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DTOFixtureTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/MockWaveFixtureTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/PodiumModelTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/PortfolioRepoStateTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/RegistryQueryTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveDetailReadingTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveSurfacePrimaryHierarchyTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WorkAttentionTests.swift","tests/fixtures/dto/metric_portfolio.json","tests/fixtures/dto/roadmap_snapshot.json","tests/fixtures/dto/wave_detail.json","wave/product/metrics/task-loop-trust.md","website/content.yaml","website/tests/e2e/test_homepage.py"]},{"sha":"2c8d4177d19163237ac54dcbfeb7bc154c660d78","title":"Main agents move off canonical main into a worktree — agents: isolate default and wave sessions in worktrees (#1230)","files":["README.md","STYLE.md","rust/loopflow/src/bin/lf.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/git.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/wave_context.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/worktrees.rs","rust/loopflow/src/flowloop/wave.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/flow.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/ops/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/run.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/git_operation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/provider_account/lease.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/README.md","rust/loopflow/src/wave/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/relocate.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave/resident.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave_host.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/wave_repository_ownership.rs"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1214,"title":"Restore Task launch from User control turns — restore runtime context for Work bodies","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-237](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-237/carry-run-execution-context-across-user-wave-and-project-runners)\n\n## Usage\n\n```console\nlf --wave product task run <issue-id>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nUser-control Task and Project launches now enter repository runtime before the hidden Work runner begins. This installs journal trace context for provider capture while preserving durable Run authority and each runner’s existing failure policy.\n\n## Changes\n\n- wrap hidden Project and Task Work entrypoints in repository runtime\n- name missing journal trace context with a typed prerequisite error\n- prove trace context exists before the injected Work body runs\n- preserve Task-fatal and Project-best-effort capture behavior\n\n## Proof\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow work_runner_entrypoint_initializes_trace_context_before_body`\n- `cargo test -p loopflow trace_capture_context_names_missing_journal_run_context`\n- `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n- a fresh User-control replay resumed LOO-234, completed kickoff, and created review-design Ask `ask_d04c0a6fe1664391a278fd4cb2b1ffb3` without the former missing-context failure","files":[],"additions":164,"deletions":55,"changed_files":8,"merge_commit":"9151b159623fc98e9f6bc0c21c78944c8e1285aa"},{"number":1216,"title":"build(deps): bump fancy-regex from 0.18.0 to 0.19.0","body":"Bumps [fancy-regex](https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex) from 0.18.0 to 0.19.0.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/releases\">fancy-regex's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.19.0</h2>\n<h3>Added</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Add <code>BytesMode</code> and the <code>RegexInput</code> trait so the matching and capture APIs can operate on strings or bytes, and allows to opt out of unicode handling if desired (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/248\">#248</a>)\n<ul>\n<li>Also allows searching within a range without slicing (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/253\">#253</a>)</li>\n<li>Assertions can be overridden at runtime, if the regex builder <code>allow_input_assertion_overrides</code> option was used (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/254\">#254</a>)</li>\n<li>Search can be performed in anchored mode, searching only at the start position specified (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/263\">#263</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Add experimental seek optimization to only invoke the VM at candidate positions where a match could occur (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/246\">#246</a>)</li>\n<li>Add <code>RegexBuilder::disallow_empty_match_at_eof_after_newline</code> to reject empty matches at the end of the haystack following a trailing newline, to match Oniguruma behavior (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/247\">#247</a>)</li>\n<li>Add <code>RegexSet</code> API for efficiently matching multiple patterns against the same text (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/255\">#255</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Changed</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Case-insensitive backreference comparison of non-ASCII text now uses direct Unicode simple case folding instead of building a regex engine per comparison, which makes patterns like <code>(?i)(\\w+)\\1</code> orders of magnitude faster on non-ASCII haystacks. The comparison is now strict fold equality over the captured text's byte-length window: a case-folded <em>prefix</em> of the window no longer counts as a match (previously a substring search could accept one and misalign the match end)</li>\n<li>Compilation performance: delegated regex-automata engines (including the whole pattern in the easy case) are now built from a directly-constructed <code>Hir</code> instead of a re-serialized pattern string, and <code>RegexSet</code> parses each pattern once for both of its set-wide engines instead of three times. This reduces compile time and peak compile memory, especially for <code>RegexSet</code> and small easy patterns</li>\n<li>Match performance: the backtracking VM's working memory (saves, backtrack stack, delegate slots) is pooled and reused across runs, making <code>is_match</code>/<code>find</code>/<code>find_iter</code> and <code>RegexSet</code> candidate verification allocation-free per call; <code>RegexSet::find_input</code> no longer allocates at failed candidate positions. Short and repeated matches improve substantially (up to ~30% for small patterns, ~10% for <code>find_iter</code> over many matches); single very long backtracking runs, where per-run setup is fully amortized anyway, can be a few percent slower</li>\n<li><code>Matches</code>, <code>CaptureMatches</code>, <code>Captures</code>, and <code>SubCaptureMatches</code> are now generic over <code>RegexInput</code>, which is a breaking change for code that named these types explicitly (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/248\">#248</a>)</li>\n<li>Patterns no longer force Unicode mode during parsing, and inline <code>(?u)</code> / <code>(?-u)</code> flags are accepted when they agree with the builder configuration</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Fixed</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Fix bug whereby inline flags were not overriding the builder options (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/issues/247\">#247</a>)</li>\n<li>Fix bug whereby <code>\\G</code> op","files":[],"additions":4,"deletions":4,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"7efbbf32c0b5ab047b37dc7c753d92a76407f2c2"},{"number":1217,"title":"build(deps-dev): bump ruff from 0.16.0 to 0.16.3 in the python group","body":"Bumps the python group with 1 update: [ruff](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff).\n\nUpdates `ruff` from 0.16.0 to 0.16.3\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/releases\">ruff's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.16.3</h2>\n<h2>Release Notes</h2>\n<p>Released on 2026-08-13.</p>\n<h3>Preview features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Fix false negatives on negative numbers (<code>PLR6104</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27251\">#27251</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>pyupgrade</code>] Add rule to replace <code>while 1</code> with <code>while True</code> (<code>UP048</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27190\">#27190</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Bug fixes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>flake8-bandit</code>] Also check keyword arguments (<code>S602</code>, <code>S603</code>, <code>S607</code>, <code>S609</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27687\">#27687</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Allow <code>continue</code> in <code>finally</code> on Python 3.8 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27626\">#27626</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Fix <code>PLE1307</code> false positive with bools (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27651\">#27651</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Fix false positives and negatives with <code>%b</code> format character (<code>PLE1300</code>, <code>PLE1307</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27560\">#27560</a>)</li>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Improve handling of concatenated strings (<code>PLE1300</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27659\">#27659</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Rule changes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>numpy</code>] Make <code>np.chararray</code> autofix backwards-compatible (<code>NPY201</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27527\">#27527</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Performance</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Enable PGO for Linux x86-64 Ruff releases (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27570\">#27570</a>)</li>\n<li>Enable PGO for Linux ARM64 Ruff releases (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27574\">#27574</a>)</li>\n<li>Enable PGO for Windows x86-64 Ruff releases (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27573\">#27573</a>)</li>\n<li>Enable PGO for macOS ARM64 Ruff releases (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27572\">#27572</a>)</li>\n<li>Reduce <code>Expr</code> size to 64 bytes (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27591\">#27591</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>CLI</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hyperlink rule codes in <code>ruff check --statistics</code> output (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27646\">#27646</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Documentation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>ruff</code>] Also suggest <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code> (<code>RUF006</code>) (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27461\">#27461</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Other changes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use mimalloc v3 (<a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/astral-sh/ruff/pull/27586\">#27586</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Contributors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Andrej730\"><code>@​Andrej730</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/alonfaraj\"><code>@​alonfaraj</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/romero-deshaw\"><code>@​romero-deshaw</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/Avasam\"><code>@​Avasam</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/tjkuson\"><code>@​tjkuson</code></a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/charliermarsh\"><code>@​charliermarsh</code></a></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">ruff's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.16.3</h2>\n<p>Released on 2026-08-13.</p>\n<h3>Preview features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>[<code>pylint</code>] Fix false negatives on negative numbers (<code>PLR6104</code>) (<a href=\"https://r","files":[],"additions":69,"deletions":69,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"1d364f2b5e567d632df4f2a01fa45603035edf85"},{"number":1218,"title":"build(deps): bump base64 from 0.22.1 to 0.23.1","body":"Bumps [base64](https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64) from 0.22.1 to 0.23.1.\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/blob/master/RELEASE-NOTES.md\">base64's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h1>0.23.1</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Make the tests build again on non-SIMD architectures</li>\n</ul>\n<h1>0.23.0</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Added more consts for preconfigured configs and engines</li>\n<li>Make DecodeError::InvalidLastSymbol more clear by including the decoded value</li>\n<li>Added SIMD-accelerated engines behind the default-on <code>simd-unsafe</code> feature: <code>Simd</code> picks the best\ninstruction set at runtime (AVX2 on <code>x86_64</code>, NEON on <code>aarch64</code>) and falls back to the scalar\n<code>GeneralPurpose</code> engine, while <code>Avx2</code> and <code>Neon</code> target one instruction set with no runtime\ndetection and work in <code>no_std</code>. The engines support the standard and URL-safe alphabets.</li>\n<li>Update MSRV to 1.71.0</li>\n<li>Add support for custom padding symbols</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/069bf7067b949f5c0a92b6ceb82492920502f2c2\"><code>069bf70</code></a> v0.23.1</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/6ab1fb0a5843013557a52c45c84b91f5d1bb87af\"><code>6ab1fb0</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/issues/310\">#310</a> from musicinmybrain/test-on-non-simd-arches</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/7cffce6f971acdf48f83112cbdd63bd61125ba06\"><code>7cffce6</code></a> Fix testing on architectures without unsafe SIMD support</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/e34f9a08c5c89a4641350ac22033f3fa4f5d4d97\"><code>e34f9a0</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/issues/308\">#308</a> from atouchet/com</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/e9240c9a01e0a4934c5619e74740caa6d1f67ce9\"><code>e9240c9</code></a> Remove outdated comment</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/9e9220a4166f628de7c8803289e120ae1e944f78\"><code>9e9220a</code></a> v0.23.0</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/870326ec592eebde9d6bfe4c5d8130c591273e9c\"><code>870326e</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/issues/306\">#306</a> from marshallpierce/mp/trailing-bits-docs</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/fbec5f1050f9fc16e6a826ebabaa2b7b0644bd67\"><code>fbec5f1</code></a> Document no trailing trailing bits</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/0a23549968f059b53cf39e96eba8f46779f322a7\"><code>0a23549</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/issues/305\">#305</a> from marshallpierce/mp/edition-2021</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/commit/f10b7e20614135aa61289140683fc93e5a45d338\"><code>f10b7e2</code></a> Update deps &amp; edition</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64/compare/v0.22.1...v0.23.1\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=base64&package-manager=cargo&previous-version=0.22.1&new-version=0.23.1)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by com","files":[],"additions":12,"deletions":6,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"347671abf7cc6a311addfa63347b812d239cc6c7"},{"number":1219,"title":"Restore roadmap and status when persisted Task values are unknown — status: preserve released Task PRs without copy","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-240](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-240/restore-roadmap-and-status-when-persisted-task-values-are-unknown)\n\n## Usage\n\n~~~sh\nlf status infrastructure --json\nlf roadmap --wave infrastructure --json\n~~~\n\n## Summary\n\nKeep status and roadmap readable when a released Task PR predates persisted reviewer-facing copy. Persisted-row validation now admits only the historical missing-copy shape, while mutation validation remains strict.\n\n## Changes\n\n- separate persisted-row admission from current Task PR mutation validation\n- add real-binary status and roadmap regressions\n- migrate a frozen 0.12.8 Task PR fixture through the current schema as semantic upgrade proof","files":[],"additions":1205,"deletions":12,"changed_files":4,"merge_commit":"6de5fd727816426b5171e7ce4fd86e3abf2eb186"},{"number":1220,"title":"LOO-249: Make Task PR copy explain intent and lifecycle","body":"<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:start -->\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Task:** [LOO-249 — Make Task PR copy explain intent and lifecycle](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-249/make-task-pr-copy-explain-intent-and-lifecycle)\n> **Task cycle:** fix\n> **PR lifecycle:** PR 1 is published for review; no Task settlement is requested.\n<!-- loopflow:task-pr-context:end -->\n\n## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow --test pr_tests configured_feature_generation_adds_task_intent_and_lifecycle_to_pr_copy\ncargo test -p loopflow --test pr_tests configured_fix_generation_names_the_task_cycle\ncargo test -p loopflow --test land_tests latest_land_disposition_wins_before_merge\n```\n\nObserve one canonical Task title, a direct Linear link, `Task cycle: feature` or `Task cycle: fix`, and a PR lifecycle sentence that says exactly what merging this PR does.\n\n## Intent\n\nGive a reviewer arriving cold the durable purpose and lifecycle of a Task PR. The copy names the user-selected Task cycle instead of exposing its three constituent flow implementations, and keeps the merge disposition next to the owning Task.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The pinned first flow identifies the durable Task cycle: `incident` means fix; other valid Task lifecycles are feature cycles.\n- Constituent flow names remain available in Task status for operational debugging, but they are not reviewer-facing intent.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Keep Task identity, cycle, PR sequence, and merge disposition machine-authored from durable state.\n- Keep evaluation guidance and implementation scope agent-authored.\n- Refresh the managed block idempotently across publish and land transitions.\n\n## Not included\n\nThis does not change Task execution or ordinary non-Task PR copy.","files":[],"additions":464,"deletions":182,"changed_files":11,"merge_commit":"c6c3df92ae712d0a0ee9658e8d064d0c684e2c80"},{"number":1221,"title":"sessions: add repo-scoped ghostty multiplexer","body":"## Usage\n\n```bash\ncd swift\n./dev run\nopen 'loopflow://asks'\n```\n\nSelect a requested session, create splits with `⌘D` or `⌘⇧D`, navigate with `⌘⌥` plus an arrow key, and run the focused tests with:\n\n```bash\n./dev test\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the requested-session popover and single terminal sheet with a repo-scoped Sessions surface in the Podium. Ask sessions now open as native Ghostty panes in a split multiplexer, keeping the human attention queue visible while preventing session operations from crossing repository boundaries.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added an immutable split-tree model and reference-owned multiplexer store with resizing, spatial focus, zoom, close/undo, pane colors, and one pane per session\n- Added a Sessions queue grouped by Wave, Project, and Task, with flow-step context, serial session preparation, failure recovery, and automatic removal of settled sessions\n- Mounted one libghostty surface per Ask or shell pane, including title and bell callbacks, bounded scrollback, and suspended rendering for hidden panes\n- Scoped Ask listing, preparation, and presentation confirmation to the repository open in the Podium\n- Made Sessions the default repository surface and added the `loopflow://asks` route, while retaining one-click navigation back to Waves and roadmap\n- Added latency instrumentation and behavioral coverage for layout operations, session reconciliation, repo scoping, serial opening, and focus behavior","files":[],"additions":2395,"deletions":472,"changed_files":17,"merge_commit":"10f8843eca995f66ee38e4515ea9500e4dfe0ce0"},{"number":1222,"title":"Preserve human reviewer mode in Task Ask sessions","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-250](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-250/preserve-human-reviewer-mode-in-task-ask-sessions)\n\n## Usage\n\n```text\n$review-design\nReviewer mode: Human reviewer.\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nUser-targeted Task FlowStep Ask sessions now tell reviewer-aware skills to run in Human reviewer mode. The Ask keeps its existing writable Task authority and explicit resolve, decline, or release settlement contract.\n\n## Changes\n\n- assign Human reviewer mode only for User-targeted flow-step Asks\n- preserve Parent-targeted Ask prompts unchanged\n- prove the real queued Task Ask rebuilds the authored review-design prompt with the assignment","files":[],"additions":19,"deletions":2,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"76717dcc87a50a3a97c5ec71509d00216f26c99f"},{"number":1223,"title":"Run the skill as a command in human-gate Ask sessions (not an inlined prompt) — ask: run human flow gates through the skill harness","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-252](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-252/run-the-skill-as-a-command-in-human-gate-ask-sessions-not-an-inlined)\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task run INF-123 --first task-design\n\n# Inside the review-design gate:\nlf ask resolve <ask-id> \"design matches intent\"\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nHuman flow gates now launch the gated skill through the same harness as `lf <skill>`, preserving its system prompt, context, configuration, and writable Task authority. This removes the generic-agent layer that could treat a human review as an ordinary prompt and self-approve it.\n\nIntervention asks remain detached, free-text conversations. Both paths continue to use the durable Ask session and require explicit resolution, decline, or release.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Dispatch `FlowStep` and `Intervention` asks through separate launch paths\n- Prepare flow gates from the skill’s real interactive harness turn\n- Append only the flow settlement contract to the skill input\n- Keep intervention providers detached from originating Work\n- Update behavioral coverage for harness-backed flow gates","files":[],"additions":74,"deletions":52,"changed_files":2,"merge_commit":"93627c753d3145a1d3f8a39f6f504b081ed45fb0"},{"number":1224,"title":"Define Project-owned live metrics for KR-driven Wave health — metrics: add project-owned live evidence","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-235](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-235/define-project-owned-live-metrics-for-kr-driven-wave-health)\n\n## Try it\n\n```bash\nuv run lf status product --json | jq '.metric_portfolio'\n\nuv run python scripts/materialize_rust_tests.py -- \\\n  cargo test -p loopflow accepted_metric_evidence_is_identical_in_status_roadmap_and_text\n```\n\nThe Product Wave exposes its reviewed metric contract through the same portfolio used by status, roadmap, prompts, and the Mac app. Without current evidence, it reports an explicit Unknown state rather than inventing a value.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR makes live metrics reviewed, Project-owned evidence for Wave decisions. Markdown contracts define meaning, targets, windows, and freshness; registered instruments publish revision-bound observations; and Rust derives one authoritative portfolio for every user surface. Metrics inform KR judgment without automatically completing KRs.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added contract validation, instrument registration, idempotent observation persistence, revision tracking, and explicit Met, Missed, Unknown, and Unavailable readings\n- Added the required `metric_portfolio` DTO to `lf status` and `lf roadmap`, plus Wave and Project agent context\n- Rendered the shared Rust-derived portfolio in the primary Mac Wave detail surface without recomputing metric state in Swift\n- Added a Product-owned Task-loop trust contract and lifecycle scorecard publisher with event-time-correct evidence\n- Updated builtin skills and documentation to place proof claims in Project KRs and official live measurement in reviewed metric contracts\n- Added cross-language fixtures and focused Python, Rust, Swift, website, and migration-materialization coverage\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- The metric-storage migration remains a release draft; source builds keep contracts visible as Unknown until it is materialized\n- This does not add alerts, raw-event analytics, external observability adapters, producer rebinding, composite Wave scores, or automatic KR completion","files":[],"additions":6253,"deletions":147,"changed_files":69,"merge_commit":"d854d79561e9115458d4a895e4d5c38dd433e1dd"},{"number":1225,"title":"Status surfaces must distinguish last recorded event from current truth — status: separate current work truth from history","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-229](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-229/status-surfaces-must-distinguish-last-recorded-event-from-current)\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf status <wave>\nlf status <wave> --json\nlf invocation list --active\n```\n\nStatus now reports current Work as `working`, `stalled`, `stopped`, or `unobservable` from fresh Home-owned evidence. Earlier failures remain separately labeled as `last failure`, and invocations without verified liveness appear as `unverified` rather than running.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR prevents historical events and stale process records from impersonating current state across the CLI, roadmap, Context Lab, and Mac app. The owning Home records tri-state Run liveness and reconciles active Runs, giving every status surface one shared projection of what is true now while preserving failures and retired Work as explicit history.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a shared `CurrentWorkObservation` model for Wave, Project, and Task state, ownership, controls, progress, and liveness evidence\n- Added bounded `present`, `absent`, and `unprovable` containment probes plus startup and periodic `lfd` reconciliation for nonterminal Runs and stranded Asks\n- Moved Project failures into timestamped `last_failure` history with Run attribution where it can be proven\n- Require fresh Home evidence before capture-ledger invocations are presented as running; otherwise surface them as unverified\n- Preserve retired Wave identities during locator replacement and keep unavailable Projects and orphaned Tasks visible in status and roadmap output\n- Updated the SQLite migration, Rust/Swift DTO fixtures, CLI rendering, Mac views, and focused behavioral coverage\n\n## Compatibility\n\nThe status DTOs replace the previous body/process fields with required current-state and liveness fields. Rust producers and Swift consumers are updated together; external JSON consumers must migrate to the new shape.","files":[],"additions":4455,"deletions":877,"changed_files":64,"merge_commit":"521ae7d3f01f1e46618ab2247ffccdf3f41411d2"},{"number":1226,"title":"Keep concurrent trace capture from killing Task Runs — trace: preserve captures under concurrent WAL writes","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-238](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-238/keep-concurrent-trace-capture-from-killing-task-runs)\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow multi_turn_capture_waits_for_concurrent_wal_writers\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPrevents concurrent SQLite WAL writers from immediately breaking multi-turn trace capture. Capture mutations now acquire write authority before reading, allowing the configured busy timeout to wait for active writers instead of failing during a deferred transaction upgrade. If persistence still fails, the invocation is finalized as partial and the Task reports an actionable, non-resumable failure while preserving its worktree and pull request.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Use immediate transactions for usage checkpoints and capture finalization, pruning, interruption, and loss\n- Finalize latched capture failures before rejecting the next Turn\n- Classify partial trace failures separately from generic resumable process failures\n- Exercise capture start, usage recording, Turn transition, and completion against an independent WAL writer\n- Record the root-cause analysis and follow-up prevention opportunities","files":[],"additions":213,"deletions":7,"changed_files":3,"merge_commit":"ebe2fb596babd13343e38b20089eb98a40cd1985"},{"number":1228,"title":"Make managed Tasks survive missing lifecycle flows — Park invalid recovery once","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-207](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-207/make-managed-tasks-survive-missing-lifecycle-flows)\n\n## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow --lib resident_records_an_invalid_lifecycle_once_without_retrying\ncargo test -p loopflow --lib lost_body_parks_an_invalid_lifecycle_without_relaunching\ncargo test -p loopflow --lib repaired_persisted_task_flows_validate_through_the_generic_path\n```\n\nThe first proof runs two resident ticks and observes one resumable failure event,\nzero Runs, and one unchanged PR. The second proves the lost-body path stops at\nthe same boundary. The third verifies that the released historical migration\nstill repairs the recorded LOO-167, LOO-193, and LOO-195 shapes from `task` to\n`slice`.\n\nBefore: every Project supervision tick could attempt the same invalid lifecycle\nrecovery again. After: the first tick writes one actionable receipt; later ticks\nwrite no event, reserve no Run, create no PR, and emit no repeated warning.\n\n## Intent\n\nMake unavailable Task lifecycle flows converge durably. Resident supervision\nnow validates the persisted plan before PR reconciliation or relaunch, records\nthe exact refusal once, and parks until the named flow becomes valid again.\n\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- One active Project supervisor owns each Task recovery loop.\n- The v0.12.5 `repair_legacy_task_flow` migration remains the sole historical\n  `task → slice` data repair.\n- Current Task lifecycle selections are immutable; unknown invalid pins are\n  restored in place or the Task is abandoned and replaced.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Reuse the launch validator instead of creating resident-only flow rules.\n- Persist one resumable Task failure so status and supervision share durable\n  evidence.\n- Compare the exact latest error and warn only when first recording it.\n- Stop before Run reservation, PR rotation, or provider launch.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Compatibility flow aliases or runtime flow-name migration.\n- Automatic worktree reconstruction.\n- Changes to Project or Wave lifecycle recovery.","files":[],"additions":123,"deletions":6,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"ab39cb83df0e8313e54300fa64fde2b950e14574"},{"number":1230,"title":"Main agents move off canonical main into a worktree — agents: isolate default and wave sessions in worktrees","body":"Linear Task: [LOO-253](https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-253/main-agents-move-off-canonical-main-into-a-worktree)\n\n## Usage\n\n```bash\n# From the canonical default branch\nlf\nlf wave product\ngit worktree list\n```\n\nBare `lf` reports the sibling worktree it moved into. Wave residents run from a stable `<repo>.wave-product` worktree, while canonical main remains clean.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoves default terminal agents and long-running Wave residents out of the canonical checkout. This prevents agent activity from dirtying the shared control plane while preserving existing local commits, tracked edits, and untracked files instead of refusing the session or discarding work.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Carries canonical-main state into an author-scoped agent worktree and resets main to the fetched default branch; deliberate non-default checkouts and existing linked worktrees remain unchanged\n- Gives each Wave resident a deterministic sibling worktree, with collision-safe names for nested Waves\n- Loads canonical skills and flow definitions while reading mutable `GOAL.md`, `MEMORY.md`, and cron state from the resident checkout\n- Restricts inline resident writes to the Wave's own goal and memory, then commits them and opens a strict CI-gated pull request\n- Blocks Wave relocation while resident changes are dirty, unpublished, or under an open PR, and syncs merged state back to canonical main before moving files\n- Adds behavioral coverage and updates the repository and Wave ownership documentation","files":[],"additions":1098,"deletions":117,"changed_files":18,"merge_commit":"2c8d4177d19163237ac54dcbfeb7bc154c660d78"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.12.12\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.12 makes Task workflow correctness part of the lifecycle itself instead of metadata that each skill must declare. Default feature work now has explicit design-review and configured-path demo gates, while Linear Task identity remains intact throughout PR operations. The result is a more reliable path from design through shipping, especially when one Task spans serial PRs.\n\n## Task workflows enforce their own lifecycle\n\nLifecycle validity is now structural, removing skill capability frontmatter as a second source of truth. The default feature path also makes its proof points explicit before work advances.\n\n- Task lifecycle validation no longer depends on capability declarations in skill frontmatter.\n- Default feature work is gated at design review and at a demo through the configured user path.\n- The built-in Task implementation and incident-prevention flows follow the structural lifecycle model.\n\n## Task identity survives PR operations\n\nPR mechanics now preserve the owning Linear Task consistently, so publishing and landing do not sever orchestration state or lose continuity between serial changes.\n\n- Task identity is retained when a PR is published, refreshed, or landed.\n- Starting the next serial PR keeps the same Task identity instead of requiring it to be reconstructed.","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":0,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":18143,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}